\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The fixed-point algebra is the averaged image}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring and let #{A} be a unital
associative #{k}-algebra. A \newvocab{symmetry} of a linear
functional #{\omega} on #{A} is a unital #{k}-algebra automorphism
#{\alpha} with #{\omega(\alpha(a))=\omega(a)} for every #{a}. Let
#{G} be a finite group, and let #{g\mapsto\alpha_g} be a
homomorphism from #{G} into the unital #{k}-algebra automorphisms
of #{A}, so that #{\alpha_{hg}=\alpha_h\circ\alpha_g}. The order
#{|G|} is a unit in #{k}. Define
##{E(a)=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\alpha_g(a).}
Write #{A^G} for the fixed-point algebra, the elements fixed by
every #{\alpha_g}. Then #{E} is linear, #{E(1)=1}, the image of
#{E} is #{A^G}, and
#{E(b_1ab_2)=b_1E(a)b_2} whenever #{b_1} and #{b_2} are fixed.
Thus #{E} is a \vocabk{normalized conditional functional}{fdip-00KV}
onto #{A^G}. If every #{\alpha_g} preserves #{\omega}, then
#{\omega(E(a))=\omega(a)}, and #{E} is a
[conditional expectation](fdip-008U) of #{\omega} onto #{A^G}.
If every #{\alpha_g} preserves an involution of #{A}, then
#{E(a^{\dagger})=E(a)^{\dagger}}. For a subgroup #{H\subseteq G}, the average
#{P_H} formed with #{H} in place of #{G} satisfies
##{P_G\circ P_H=P_G.}}

\p{When the automorphisms are conjugations
#{\alpha_g(R)=\rho(g)R\rho(g)^{-1}} by a representation
#{\rho} on a finite-dimensional space, the fixed-point algebra
is the commutant of #{\rho(G)}. For the real half-turn, let
#{P} be its operator and let #{E_\pm=2^{-1}(\bbI\pm P)} be the
represented projectors of the \vocabk{half-turn}{fdip-000L}. The
average of conjugation by #{P} is
##{E(R)=E_+RE_++E_-RE_-.}
The sector matrix of this operator is the calculation of
\ref{fdip-00L3}.}

\p{The neutral [Gauss constraint](fdip-008A) for a finite gauge
group asks for the link functions fixed by every group element,
and its projector is this same average. An invariant functional
therefore satisfies #{\omega=\omega\circ E}: its value depends
on an observable only through the averaged observable. The
charged space remains the intertwiner space of
\ref{fdip-008B}. The multiplicity matrices of a real
orthogonal action remain the commutant description of
\ref{fdip-004Z}.}

\proof{
\p{Each #{\alpha_g} is #{k}-linear, so #{E} is linear. A unital
automorphism fixes #{1}, and therefore #{E(1)=1}.}

\p{For #{h\in G},
##{\alpha_h(E(a))
=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\alpha_h(\alpha_g(a))
=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\alpha_{hg}(a).}
As #{g} runs through #{G}, so does #{hg}. Hence
#{\alpha_h(E(a))=E(a)}, and the image of #{E} lies in #{A^G}.}

\p{If #{b} is fixed by every #{\alpha_g}, every summand equals
#{b}, so #{E(b)=b}. Thus #{A^G} is contained in the image, and
#{E} is the identity on #{A^G}. The two inclusions give
##{A^G=\operatorname{im}E.}}

\p{Let #{b_1} and #{b_2} be fixed. Multiplicativity gives
#{\alpha_g(b_1ab_2)=\alpha_g(b_1)\alpha_g(a)\alpha_g(b_2)
=b_1\alpha_g(a)b_2}. Averaging both sides yields
#{E(b_1ab_2)=b_1E(a)b_2}.}

\p{If every #{\alpha_g} preserves #{\omega}, then
#{\omega(\alpha_g(a))=\omega(a)}. Averaging the scalar values
gives #{\omega(E(a))=\omega(a)}. If every automorphism preserves
an involution, #{\alpha_g(a^{\dagger})=\alpha_g(a)^{\dagger}}, and the same
average gives #{E(a^{\dagger})=E(a)^{\dagger}}.}

\p{For #{H\subseteq G} and #{h\in H}, right multiplication by #{h}
permutes #{G}. Therefore
##{P_GP_H(a)
=\frac1{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\alpha_{gh}(a)
=P_G(a).}
The reindexing uses the group law only.}

\p{For a conjugation action, #{\alpha_h(E(R))=E(R)} is the
statement that #{E(R)} commutes with every #{\rho(h)}. If #{R}
already lies in the commutant, then #{E(R)=R}, because #{E} fixes
every invariant element. Thus the image is exactly the commutant.}

\p{Since #{P^2=\bbI}, the two conjugates are #{R} and #{PRP}, and
##{E(R)=\frac12(R+PRP).}
Expanding the projectors gives
##{E_+RE_++E_-RE_-
=\frac14\bigl((\bbI+P)R(\bbI+P)+(\bbI -P)R(\bbI -P)\bigr)
=\frac12(R+PRP).}
The two formulas agree.}
}

\p{No norm on #{A} is used, and the average is not asserted to be
a norm-one projection. The argument gives the finite average
only. It does not identify the sectors with the center of the
commutant, and it does not replace the charged intertwiner
description.}
